WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.534
Google Cloud CLI scripts often run in
CI/CD systems, scheduled jobs,

2
00:00:06.614 --> 00:00:12.335
deployment agents, containers, and
operational workstations.

3
00:00:12.415 --> 00:00:20.009
Each environment has different credential,
logging, network, and runtime behavior.

4
00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:24.354
Pipeline identities should be scoped to
the job,

5
00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:31.716
and production paths should be protected
with branch, tag, release, environment,

6
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:33.564
and approval gates.

7
00:00:33.644 --> 00:00:41.342
Workload Identity Federation and other
runtime identity patterns can reduce reliance

8
00:00:41.422 --> 00:00:47.768
on stored service account keys, but they
still require careful scoping.

9
00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:53.361
A low-trust trigger should not receive
production authority.

10
00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:56.770
Validation, build, publish, deploy,

11
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:02.884
and rollback jobs should have permissions
that match their purpose,

12
00:01:02.964 --> 00:01:10.350
and sensitive environments should have
technical gates that align with the release

13
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.262
process.

14
00:01:11.342 --> 00:01:17.480
Operational logs should identify the
automation identity, project,

15
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:25.049
region or zone, target category, action,
time, and result without exposing secrets.

16
00:01:25.129 --> 00:01:31.059
Cloud Audit Logs, identity logs, service
logs, monitoring alerts,

17
00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:36.652
and access reviews help teams understand
what automation did.

18
00:01:36.732 --> 00:01:40.997
Monitoring should look for unusual
identities,

19
00:01:41.077 --> 00:01:43.990
unexpected projects or regions,

20
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:50.000
high-impact actions outside release
windows, repeated failures,

21
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.842
unexpected IAM changes, and service
behavior that differs from the expected

22
00:01:56.922 --> 00:01:58.794
deployment outcome.

23
00:01:58.874 --> 00:02:03.659
Incident response should cover credential
exposure,

24
00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:09.565
wrong-project changes, unexpected IAM
changes, failed changes,

25
00:02:09.645 --> 00:02:12.974
and rollback or containment actions.

26
00:02:13.054 --> 00:02:17.839
Teams should know how to revoke or rotate
credentials,

27
00:02:17.919 --> 00:02:25.097
pause affected workflows, identify
impacted resources, review audit evidence,

28
00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:27.257
and update guardrails.

29
00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:34.099
Incidents and near misses should lead to
better validation, narrower roles,

30
00:02:34.179 --> 00:02:38.340
safer logging, and stronger review
processes.
